A||||4||sully5||alternator bolts||||8||
Z||000000||sully5||04-05-2000||09:55 PM||||Is it possible to remove the alternator bracket with the alternator still attached, and engine still in car? Discovered that the bracket was busted on Monday. Got a new one off ebay yesterday. went out to remove the old one tonight. everything was going great until i got to the top bolt. It snapped off about 2 inches down. Very frustrating. Thanks for letting me blow off some steam, and thanks in advance for any help. BTW - the car is an 86 V6 & automatic.||||reg||8
Z||000001||BAM-BAM||04-06-2000||01:01 AM||||  You might be able to if you disconnect the rear tierod end at the back and bottom of the rear hub. I believe it takes a 19mm. remove cotter pin, run the nut down till there is no thread exposed. tap the nut "lightly" with a hammer till it breaks loose. Remove the nut and pull the rod out of the way. this "MIGHT" give you enough room. I usually just remove the alternator first.<P> Good Luck, BAM-BAM ||||reg||12
Z||000002||Standard||04-06-2000||02:14 AM||||Don't forget to mark the position of the nut before you take it off, otherwise you'll have to get it aligned..||||reg||1
Z||000003||WKDFIRO||04-06-2000||02:21 AM||||Tapping the side with a hammer is the best way of taking off the tie rod end but since you are only taking off the nut and not doing anything with the tie rod itself it shouldn't be a problem. Automatic with an alternator...thats the worst! IF all else fails and you cannot get it out anyother way. Take a 15 mm socket and undo the rear cradle bolts (the ones closest to the back of the car.) with a jack underneath and the car in the air. Slowly lower the cradle down so you can access the alternator easier. <P>Good luck.<P>PS You can hammer the living CRAP out of that tie rod knuckle and it won't break. Tried it in a junk yard with a LARGE ball pean hammer to find out and it works in the shop. If it creeps you out using that much force than tapping will do fine too.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Phil||04-06-2000||07:24 AM||||You could also finish busting up your already broken bracket and remove it in pieces||||reg||1
